= Module 4: Census Urban Hierarchy & Spatial Growth

== 4.1 Census Definition of an "Urban Area" in India
In India, the Census adopts a dual definition to delineate urban settlements from rural settlements:

#definition-box(title: "Statutory Towns versus Census Towns")[
  - *1. Statutory Towns:*
    - Any place with a municipal corporation, municipality, cantonment board, or notified town area committee established under state or central legislation, regardless of its demographic size or occupational profile.
  - *2. Census Towns (Non-Statutory Urban Centers):*
    - Any rural settlement that satisfies all three of the following empirical criteria simultaneously:
      1. A minimum aggregate population of *5,000 persons*;
      2. At least *75% of the male main working population* engaged in non-agricultural pursuits;
      3. A population density of at least *400 persons per square kilometer* (approx. 1,000 persons per sq. mile).
]

== 4.2 The Six-Fold Population Size Hierarchy
The Census of India classifies all urban settlements into six population size classes:
- *Class I Towns / Cities:* Population of *1,00,000 and above*. (Cities with population $>= 1$ lakh are formally designated as *"Cities"*).
- *Class II Towns:* Population between *50,000 and 99,999*.
- *Class III Towns:* Population between *20,000 and 49,999*.
- *Class IV Towns:* Population between *10,000 and 19,999*.
- *Class V Towns:* Population between *5,000 and 9,999*.
- *Class VI Towns:* Population *less than 5,000*.

#note-box(title: "Metropolitan Cities, Mega-Cities, and Urban Agglomerations")[
  - *Metropolitan (Million-Plus) Cities:* Population between 10 to 50 lakh; count grew from 35 (2001) to *53 cities (2011)* (only 1 in 1901, 5 in 1951).
  - *Mega-Cities:* Population exceeding 50 lakh (5 million); Greater Mumbai (18.4 M — largest UA), Delhi (16.3 M), Kolkata (14.1 M), Chennai (8.6 M), Bengaluru (8.5 M), Hyderabad (7.7 M).
  - *Urban Agglomeration (UA):* Continuous urban spread of a core city/town with adjoining outgrowths (OGs) such as railway colonies, port areas, and university campuses.
]

== 4.3 The Phenomenon of "Top-Heavy" Urbanization
A defining structural characteristic of Indian urbanization is its sharply unbalanced, *"top-heavy"* distribution. Although India has thousands of small and medium towns (Classes II to VI), the aggregate urban population is massively concentrated within Class I cities:
- *Concentration of Urban Population:* Class I cities (population $>= 1$ lakh) account for over *60% of India's total urban populace*.
- *Marginalization of Small Towns:* Classes IV, V, and VI towns together account for less than 10% of the aggregate urban population, suffering from chronic infrastructural underinvestment, economic stagnation, and lack of secondary employment generation.

