= Module 6: Urban Problems, Slum Geography & Unit Synthesis

== 6.1 Critical Challenges of Rapid Indian Urbanization
While urban centers generate over 60% of India's GDP, rapid and unplanned urban growth has spawned critical spatial and ecological crises:
- *Severe Housing Deficits & Proliferation of Slums:* Skyrocketing urban land prices force millions of low-income rural-to-urban migrants into informal squatter colonies lacking basic civic infrastructure.
- *Water Scarcity & Drainage Stress:* Rapid aquifer depletion, encroachment of urban wetlands, and clogged drainage networks trigger severe monsoon flash floods.
- *Municipal Solid Waste Crises:* Indian cities generate over 1.5 lakh tonnes of waste daily; unscientific open dumps (Ghazipur, Deonar) release toxic leachate into groundwater.
- *Atmospheric Pollution & Gridlock:* Skyrocketing vehicle ownership causes traffic congestion and hazardous PM2.5/PM10 air pollution across North Indian cities.

#analogy-box(title: "Case Study in Urban Geography: Dharavi Slum, Mumbai")[
  Located in Mumbai, *Dharavi* is one of Asia's most prominent informal settlements:
  - *Spatial Form:* Over 7 to 10 lakh inhabitants squeezed over 2.1 km² (density >3,00,000/km²); precarious tenements separated by 1-meter alleys and open drains.
  - *Informal Economic Dynamo:* Vibrant \$1B informal economy with 5,000+ micro-enterprises in leather tanning, plastic recycling, garment embroidery, and pottery (Kumbharwada).
  - *Civic Vulnerability:* Severe sanitation deficits (hundreds sharing a single public toilet), toxic recycling fumes, and high vulnerability to waterborne epidemics.
]

